Doctor: Good morning. What brings you in today?

Patient: I've had a cough and sore throat for about five days. The cough started dry but now I occasionally cough up a small amount of clear mucus. It is worse at night and has been disturbing my sleep.

Doctor: Have you had fever or difficulty breathing?

Patient: I had a mild fever of about 100.5 degrees Fahrenheit two days ago, but I don't have a fever today. I don't have difficulty breathing or chest pain.

Doctor: Any nasal symptoms?

Patient: Yes, I have mild nasal congestion and a runny nose. I also had a sore throat for the first two days, but it is getting better.

Doctor: Have you taken anything for these symptoms?

Patient: I took paracetamol 500 milligrams once when I had the fever. It helped. I've also taken an over-the-counter cough syrup, which gives me temporary relief.

Doctor: Do you have any medical conditions or take regular medications?

Patient: No. I'm generally healthy and I don't take any regular medicines. I also don't have any known drug allergies.

Doctor: Do you smoke or vape?

Patient: No, I don't smoke or vape.

Doctor: Have you been around anyone who was sick recently?

Patient: Yes. A coworker had a cough last week and we were working together in the same room.

Doctor: Did you take a COVID test?

Patient: Yes, I took a home rapid test two days ago and it was negative.

Doctor: Let me check your vital signs. Your temperature is 98.6 degrees Fahrenheit, blood pressure is 120 over 78, pulse is 80 beats per minute, and oxygen saturation is 98% on room air.

Doctor: Your lungs are clear bilaterally with no wheezing or crackles. Your throat has mild redness, and your nasal passages are mildly congested.

Doctor: Based on your symptoms and examination, this is most consistent with an uncomplicated viral upper respiratory infection with an acute cough. There are no signs today suggesting pneumonia or another serious respiratory infection.

Patient: Do I need antibiotics?

Doctor: No, antibiotics are not indicated at this time because your symptoms and examination are consistent with a viral infection.

Patient: What should I do for the cough?

Doctor: Continue drinking plenty of fluids, use warm liquids to soothe your throat, and get adequate rest. You may continue the over-the-counter cough medicine if it provides relief, following the dosage instructions on the package.

Patient: Should I continue taking paracetamol?

Doctor: Only if you develop fever or significant body aches. You do not need to take it routinely while you are feeling well.

Patient: When should I come back?

Doctor: Seek medical attention if you develop difficulty breathing, persistent or severe chest pain, coughing up blood, confusion, or a high or persistent fever. If the cough lasts longer than three weeks or becomes significantly worse, return for reassessment.

Doctor: For now, continue supportive care and monitor your symptoms. You do not need antibiotics or prescription medication at this time.

Patient: Okay, thank you doctor.

Doctor: You're welcome. Take care.
